  "content": {
    "id": "p52-q32-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "32",
    "prompt": "Two planes have the same size fuel tanks, but one will travel for 21 hours on a full tank and the other 35 hours on a full tank. They set off at the same time with full tanks and both planes burn fuel at a constant rate. After how long will one have 3 times as much fuel as the other?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "17.5 hours",
      "worked": "Let A be the plane whose full tank lasts 21 hours and B the plane whose full tank lasts 35 hours. Take each tank's capacity as 105 units, the LCM of 21 and 35. A burns 5 units per hour and B burns 3 units per hour.\n\nLet $t$ be the time in hours and $F_A,F_B$ the remaining fuel amounts.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\nF_A &= 105-5t \\\\\nF_B &= 105-3t\n\\end{align*}$$\n\nB burns fuel more slowly, so set $F_B=3F_A$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\n105-3t &= 3(105-5t) \\\\\n105-3t &= 315-15t \\\\\n12t &= 210 \\\\\nt &= 17.5\n\\end{align*}$$\n\nAfter 17.5 hours, B has three times as much fuel as A.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 90
  },
